{"name":"Blue Indigo","hex":"#535a7c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"indigo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A purplish-blue color.","A greenish dark blue color; the color of indigo dye."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["having a deep purplish-blue colour"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/indigo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Indigo","blocks":[{"text":"Indigo is a term used for a number of hues in the region of blue. The word comes from the ancient dye of the same name. The term \"indigo\" can refer to the color of the dye, various colors of fabric dyed with indigo dye, a spectral color, one of the seven colors of the rainbow as described by Sir Isaac Newton, or a region on the color wheel, and can include various shades of blue, ultramarine, and green-blue. Since the web era, the term has also been used for various purple and violet hues identified as \"indigo\", based on use of the term \"indigo\" in HTML web page specifications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Indian_indigo_dye_lump.jpg/330px-Indian_indigo_dye_lump.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigo","wikidata":"Q5967009"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Passerina_cyanea_Sam_Smith_Park_Toronto.jpg/500px-Passerina_cyanea_Sam_Smith_Park_Toronto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Passerina_cyanea_Sam_Smith_Park_Toronto.jpg/500px-Passerina_cyanea_Sam_Smith_Park_Toronto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APasserina_cyanea_Sam_Smith_Park_Toronto.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Lactarius_indigo_%28Schwein.%29_Fr_939963.jpg/500px-Lactarius_indigo_%28Schwein.%29_Fr_939963.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Lactarius_indigo_%28Schwein.%29_Fr_939963.jpg/500px-Lactarius_indigo_%28Schwein.%29_Fr_939963.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALactarius_indigo_(Schwein.)_Fr_939963.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/1923ca_Quarter_anna_Bundi_indigo_SG26a_Mi18.jpg/500px-1923ca_Quarter_anna_Bundi_indigo_SG26a_Mi18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars